  "remedy_content": {
    "body_markdown": "# Verify Before Claiming\n\n## When this applies\nBefore stating that a fix, test suite, lint pass, build, check, repair, or release is complete or passing, the agent must have actually run the relevant verification command in this session and observed its output. This applies whether the claim is about a whole task or a small sub-step.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Identify the concrete verification command implied by the task (test suite, lint, build, release check, etc.). If none is specified, infer it from project config (CI file, package.json scripts, README) or ask.\n2. Run it in-session.\n3. Report the literal result — pass/fail counts, exit code, or a relevant output snippet — not a paraphrase like \"it works\" or \"looks good.\"\n4. If the command cannot be run (missing credentials, unavailable environment, out of scope for this turn), say so explicitly instead of asserting success or completion. Never imply verification happened when it didn't.\n5. If a prior run's result is being reused, say so and give its age/context — don't present a stale or cached result as freshly confirmed.\n\n## Anti-patterns to avoid\n- \"The fix is complete\" / \"tests pass\" / \"lint is clean\" / \"the check passed\" with no command actually run this session.\n- Inferring success purely from reading the diff or code change.\n- Treating a skipped, partial, or previously-run check as equivalent to a fresh pass.\n- Softening a hedge (\"should work\") into a completion claim (\"it works\") without ever closing the gap by running something.\n\n## Examples\n- Bad: \"Fixed the bug, all tests pass.\"\n- Good: \"Changed X; ran `npm test` — 42 passed, 0 failed (log attached).\"\n- Good (blocked): \"Made the change but couldn't run the test suite here (no runner configured) — verification is still outstanding, flagging before you rely on this.\"",
    "description": "Use when about to claim that a fix, test, lint, build, check, or repair passed, or that a task/release is complete — run the actual verification command first and report only the observed result.",
    "name": "fake-verify-before-claim"
  },
  "remedy_type": "skill",
  "routing_rationale": "All 10 promoted incidents (harbor, cinder, fake-aurora, fake-cinder, fake-harbor, fake-lantern) share one failure mode: the agent asserted a fix/test/lint/check/release/repair was complete or passing without having actually run the verification command in-session. This is a direct hit on R1 (skill fake-verify-before-claim, \"Use when a completion claim requires verification\") — same trigger, same phase (claiming completion vs. verifying it). Merge bias is law, so this is a revision of R1, not a new sibling. R2 (Fake Release Discipline) is a narrower claude.md rule scoped only to running a release's declared verification command before reporting a release complete; it doesn't cover the broader set seen here (tests, lint, generic \"checks\", repairs), so it stays clear and untouched — R1 is the correct, broader revision target. R3/R4 are about honoring stated scope/constraints during the work itself, a different phase and trigger entirely, so both are clear. Because the existing skill R1 already targets this exact failure and the cluster kept recurring across six projects, the fix is to strengthen/broaden R1's body (explicit procedure, anti-patterns, and coverage beyond just \"tests\" to lint/build/release/repair) rather than downgrade to a claude.md one-liner — the prior attempt's validation error was purely a missing-field issue (name/description/body_markdown), not a wrong remedy type, so remedy_type stays skill to match the R1 it revises.",
